SOLUTION

This is a typical KodeKloud Jenkins lab. Since Jenkins needs to copy files from one remote server (stapp01) to another remote server (Storage Server), the easiest solution is to use SSH in a Jenkins freestyle job.

Step 1: Log in to Jenkins
Click Jenkins
Username: admin
Password: Adm!n321
Step 2: Install Required Plugin (if missing)

Go to

Manage Jenkins → Plugins → Available Plugins

Install:

SSH Agent Plugin 
and 
Publish Over SSH

(or if the lab specifically requires it, install Publish Over SSH.)

Restart Jenkins if prompted.

In these KodeKloud/Stratos Jenkins labs, Configure SSH servers means telling Jenkins how to connect to the remote servers. Here's how to do it.

1. Open the SSH configuration

In Jenkins:

Manage Jenkins
System
Scroll down to Publish over SSH

If you don't see Publish over SSH, install the Publish Over SSH plugin first and restart Jenkins.

2. Configure the SSH Key

In the Publish over SSH section:

Check Disable exec: No (leave unchecked)
In Key, paste the private SSH key that Jenkins should use.

If the lab has already generated a key, you can usually find it on the Jenkins server:

sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/.ssh/id_rsa

Copy the entire contents, including:

-----BEGIN OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY-----
...
-----END OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY-----

Paste it into the Key field.

3. Add the App Server

Click Add under SSH Servers.

Fill in:

Name: stapp03
Hostname: stapp03
Username: tony (or whatever user your lab specifies)
Remote Directory: leave blank

Click Test Configuration.

If it succeeds, save it.

4. Add the Storage Server

Click Add again.

Fill in:

Name: ststor01
Hostname: ststor01
Username: natasha (or the lab's storage user)
Remote Directory: /usr/src/itadmin

Click Test Configuration.
verify the directory and permissions in storage server
SSH into the storage server:

ssh natasha@ststor01

(or your storage server username)

Then run:

ls -ld /usr/src/itadmin

You will probably see one of these situations:

Case 1: Directory does not exist

Example:

ls: cannot access '/usr/src/itadmin': No such file or directory

Create it:

sudo mkdir -p /usr/src/itadmin
sudo chmod 777 /usr/src/itadmin
Case 2: Directory exists but permissions are wrong

Example:

drwx------ root root /usr/src/itadmin

Fix permissions:

sudo chmod 777 /usr/src/itadmin

or change ownership:

sudo chown natasha:natasha /usr/src/itadmin
Configure Jenkins SSH key access
1. Become the Jenkins user

On the Jenkins server:

sudo su - jenkins

Create the SSH directory:

mkdir -p ~/.ssh
chmod 700 ~/.ssh
2. Generate an SSH key

Run:

ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096

Press Enter for all prompts:

Enter file in which to save the key:

Press Enter.

Enter passphrase:

Press Enter (empty).

3. Copy the key to stapp01

Run:

ssh-copy-id tony@stapp01

Enter the tony password when prompted.

You should see:

Number of key(s) added: 1
4. Copy the key to the storage server

Run:

ssh-copy-id natasha@ststor01

Enter the storage password.

5. Test passwordless SSH

Still as the Jenkins user:

ssh tony@stapp01

It should enter directly without asking for a password.

Then:

ssh natasha@ststor01

should also work without a password.
Create a Freestyle project named:
copy-logs
Under Build Triggers, check Build periodically and use:
*/11 * * * *
Under Build → Execute shell, use:
#!/bin/bash

# Copy Apache logs to /tmp on App Server 3
ssh -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no tony@stapp03 "sudo cp /var/log/httpd/access_log /tmp/access_log && sudo cp /var/log/httpd/error_log /tmp/error_log && sudo chmod 644 /tmp/access_log /tmp/error_log"

# Copy logs from App Server 3 to Jenkins
scp -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no tony@stapp03:/tmp/access_log /tmp/
scp -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no tony@stapp03:/tmp/error_log /tmp/

# Copy logs from Jenkins to the Storage Server
scp -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no /tmp/access_log natasha@ststor01:/usr/src/itadmin/
scp -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no /tmp/error_log natasha@ststor01:/usr/src/itadmin/


